WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.522
Elmi Guray Academy.

2
00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:04.470
Lesson 3.

3
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.690
Main AI Tools for Developers.

4
00:00:07.852 --> 00:00:16.552
Technical Explanation — English Many AI services are available to developers, but they do not all solve the same problem.

5
00:00:16.502 --> 00:00:22.507
Some AI products are designed to work beside the developer inside a code editor.

6
00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:28.879
Other products attempt to generate most of an application automatically from a short description.

7
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:45.999
This EGA course focuses mainly on tools that help developers improve their normal coding workflow: GitHub Copilot Cursor ChatGPT These tools can help a developer work faster, understand code, explore solutions, and improve existing work.

8
00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:53.431
However, the developer must remain responsible for reviewing, testing, and approving the final result.

9
00:00:53.431 --> 00:01:00.211
Two Approaches to AI Development AI development services can be placed into two broad groups.

10
00:01:00.211 --> 00:01:09.531
Approach Main Idea Developer Role AI-assisted development AI helps the developer write, understand, edit, and review code.

11
00:01:09.531 --> 00:01:12.996
The developer stays actively involved.

12
00:01:12.996 --> 00:01:20.319
AI-generated applications AI attempts to create large parts of an application from instructions.

13
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:24.626
The developer reviews and adjusts the generated result.

14
00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:32.398
AI-Assisted Development AI-assisted development means using AI while still controlling the programming process.

15
00:01:32.398 --> 00:01:37.779
The developer may ask AI to: Complete an unfinished line of code.

16
00:01:37.779 --> 00:01:40.863
Explain an unfamiliar function.

17
00:01:40.863 --> 00:01:43.540
Suggest a better solution.

18
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.107
Find a possible mistake.

19
00:01:46.107 --> 00:01:48.825
Improve code organization.

20
00:01:48.825 --> 00:01:51.284
Create documentation.

21
00:01:51.284 --> 00:01:53.635
Help prepare tests.

22
00:01:53.635 --> 00:01:57.806
This method combines human knowledge with AI speed.

23
00:01:57.806 --> 00:02:05.374
AI Application Builders Some services try to build complete applications from natural-language instructions.

24
00:02:05.374 --> 00:02:14.491
Examples of services in this wider category may include tools such as Replit, Bolt, v0, and similar platforms.

25
00:02:14.491 --> 00:02:24.260
A user may enter an instruction such as: Create a student-registration website with a form, dashboard, payment page, and database.

26
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:39.423
The service may attempt to generate: Page layouts User-interface components Application logic Database connections Deployment settings These services may be helpful for quick prototypes, experiments, and simple applications.

27
00:02:39.423 --> 00:02:50.537
They may become more difficult to manage when a project requires detailed business rules, security, payments, database permissions, or several connected features.

28
00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:59.491
Why Complex Projects Require More Control A generated application can look correct while still containing serious hidden problems.

29
00:02:59.491 --> 00:03:24.939
Possible problems include: Incorrect database permissions Weak authentication Missing error handling Incorrect payment calculations Duplicate records Broken mobile layouts Code that is difficult to maintain Features that do not match the real requirements EGA Development Principle The more important the application becomes, the more carefully the developer must understand and verify every part of it.

30
00:03:24.939 --> 00:03:32.561
Why GitHub Copilot Is a Main Focus GitHub Copilot can work directly inside supported code editors.

31
00:03:32.561 --> 00:03:38.295
It can examine nearby code and suggest what the developer may want to write next.

32
00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:52.738
GitHub Copilot may help with: Code completion Repeated code patterns Function suggestions Code explanations Test suggestions Documentation The developer can accept, modify, or reject every suggestion.

33
00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:59.260
Why Cursor Is a Main Focus Cursor is a code editor built around AI-assisted development.

34
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:16.828
Its AI tools can work with: One selected line A complete file Several related files A larger project structure Refactoring requests Questions about existing code This deeper project context can be useful when a coding task involves more than one small code example.

35
00:04:16.828 --> 00:04:25.442
Why ChatGPT Is Included ChatGPT is useful for development tasks that are not limited to editing one code file.

36
00:04:25.442 --> 00:04:47.140
A developer may use it for: Planning a project Learning technical concepts Comparing possible solutions Understanding an error message Preparing documentation Discussing database structure Creating exercises and learning materials ChatGPT can be helpful when the developer needs a broad discussion before making a technical decision.

37
00:04:47.140 --> 00:05:00.510
Choosing a Tool Based on the Task Development Task Possible Tool Completing code while typing GitHub Copilot Updating several connected files Cursor Planning project architecture ChatGPT or Cursor

38
00:05:00.676 --> 00:05:11.726
Creating a quick prototype An AI application builder Checking production security Developer knowledge supported by AI No single tool is best for every situation.

39
00:05:11.676 --> 00:05:19.013
A developer should choose based on the task, project size, required control, and possible risks.

40
00:05:19.013 --> 00:05:23.334
A Responsible AI Workflow Understand the problem.

41
00:05:23.334 --> 00:05:26.065
Choose the most suitable tool.

42
00:05:26.065 --> 00:05:29.923
Give clear instructions and useful context.

43
00:05:29.923 --> 00:05:32.518
Read the generated result.

44
00:05:32.518 --> 00:05:35.806
Check whether it matches the requirements.

45
00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:37.749
Test the code.

46
00:05:37.749 --> 00:05:41.160
Correct errors and security problems.

47
00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.176
Save only the verified version.

48
00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:50.440
Efficiency means reducing unnecessary work without removing careful technical review.

49
00:05:50.440 --> 00:06:00.412
HTML Learning in This Lesson This lesson uses real HTML elements so that students can learn both AI development and webpage structure.

50
00:06:00.412 --> 00:06:16.105
Important elements used in this lesson include: <blockquote> <article> <table> <thead> <tbody> <tr> <th> <td> The Blockquote Element The <blockquote> element displays a longer quotation or an important statement separately from the surrounding paragraphs.

51
00:06:16.105 --> 00:06:21.595
HTML Code <blockquote> AI should support the developer's knowledge.

52
00:06:21.595 --> 00:06:27.206
/blockquote> Browser Output AI should support the developer's knowledge.

53
00:06:27.206 --> 00:06:30.032
blockquote> is the opening tag.

54
00:06:30.032 --> 00:06:32.695
The sentence is the content.

55
00:06:32.695 --> 00:06:35.997
/blockquote> is the closing tag.

56
00:06:35.997 --> 00:06:42.627
The Article Element The <article> element groups complete content that can make sense independently.

57
00:06:42.627 --> 00:06:51.119
HTML Code <article> <h3>Important Principle</h3> <p> Developers must test AI-generated code.

58
00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:55.494
/p> </article> The opening tag is <article> .

59
00:06:55.494 --> 00:06:58.578
The closing tag is </article> .

60
00:06:58.578 --> 00:07:05.684
It is useful for an independent explanation, lesson, article, news item, or blog post.

61
00:07:05.684 --> 00:07:12.546
HTML Table Structure A table organizes related information into rows and columns.

62
00:07:12.546 --> 00:07:31.893
table border="1"> <thead> <tr> <th>Tool</th> <th>Purpose</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Cursor</td> <td>AI-supported code editing</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table> creates the complete table.

63
00:07:31.893 --> 00:07:34.950
thead> contains the table headings.

64
00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.551
tbody> contains the main table information.

65
00:07:38.551 --> 00:07:41.024
tr> creates a row.

66
00:07:41.024 --> 00:07:43.959
th> creates a heading cell.

67
00:07:43.959 --> 00:07:47.124
td> creates a normal data cell.

68
00:07:47.124 --> 00:07:56.839
Browser Output Tool Purpose Cursor AI-supported code editing Image Path Reminder The EGA logo is displayed with the <img> element.

69
00:07:56.839 --> 00:08:09.230
img src="images/ega-logo.png" alt="Elmi Guray Academy logo" width="180" > src identifies the image location.

70
00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.744
alt describes the image.

71
00:08:11.744 --> 00:08:14.842
width controls its displayed width.

72
00:08:14.842 --> 00:08:22.383
The correct path is images/ega-logo.png because the file is inside the images folder.

73
00:08:22.383 --> 00:08:26.825
Important Rules Choose an AI tool according to the task.

74
00:08:26.825 --> 00:08:30.806
Do not assume that one tool can solve every problem.

75
00:08:30.806 --> 00:08:34.325
Review generated code before using it.

76
00:08:34.325 --> 00:08:39.380
Test databases, payments, forms, and authentication.

77
00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.967
Do not use code that you do not understand.

78
00:08:42.967 --> 00:08:46.581
Keep control of the final development decision.

79
00:08:46.581 --> 00:08:51.703
Never share passwords, private keys, or secret credentials.

80
00:08:51.703 --> 00:08:55.942
Use AI to strengthen learning, not avoid learning.

81
00:08:55.942 --> 00:09:19.611
Common Beginner Mistakes Accepting every AI suggestion automatically Believing a generated application is always complete Failing to test forms and database operations Choosing a popular tool that does not fit the task Giving instructions without enough context Ignoring security and maintenance Depending on AI without learning programming Using an incorrect image path

